
Message Base Libraries


1.0 An introduction


Message Base Libraries allow foreign message base standards to be used
seamlessly with every aspect of your Zeus BBS. They operate under the
principle that Zeus does not know, or need to know how the message data
is stored.

This means that the message base specifications could be extended to
allow the reading of a seperate message base over a network, or simply
another message base standard.

Zeus comes with two message base libraries:

 - mbzeus.library
 - mbstarmsg.library

The first, mbzeus.library is the most commonly used message base library
as it uses a fast, indexed, asynchronous and buffered "three file per
area" format. This means that message base files do not fragment the
hard drive to a great extent, and unnecessary space is not wasted on
the storage medium with small files. AmigaDos files take up an extra
512 bytes of space regardless of their length. The one main disadvantage
of the mbzeus.library is that it is proprietary to Zeus. Other programs
cannot take advantage of it unless they are programmed directly for
Zeus.

To counter this, we also supply the mbstarmsg.library with Zeus. The
mbstarmsg.library, the C source of which is supplied in this development
archive is a slower, more cumbersome standard. Its great advantage
is that many Amiga mail processors, readers, Areafix and other utility
programs have direct support for this standard.

The "starmsg" base is so called because the name of each mail file is
its number followed by the ".MSG" suffix, for example the third message
in area AMIGASOFT might be stored in "MAIL:AMIGASOFT/3.MSG".


2.0 Implementing a message base library


A message base library is a standard Amiga shared library, as defined in
the file "Developers/Include/mb_lib.h". You must create your library with
this exact template, as MsgControl calls these functions relating to their
offset, not name.

Functions in message base libraries are NEVER called directly. Only the
zmf.library and mbManager will ever contact your library directly. All
other Zeus programs work from a link with the similarly named functions
in the zmf.library, e.g. LoadMessage() links to mbLoadMessage().


2.0.1 Initialisation

struct MsgBaseLink * mbInitialise ( struct MsgGlobals * ) ;
int                  mbGetInfo    ( struct MsgInfo * ) ;
BOOL                 mbInitBase   ( struct msgarea * ) ;
void                 mbQuit       ( void ) ;

Functions in the message base library will only be called while MsgControl
is active. Your library will be activated and deactivated when MsgControl
opens and closes with the functions "mbInitialiase" and "mbQuit" respectively.

The function "mbInitBase" must be available at all times, and should return
some information about the library into the "struct MsgInfo *" passed to
the function.

A MsgInfo structure contains the following elements with example entries
in comments.

struct	MsgInfo
{
	char	Name[40];     /* mbstarmsg.library v1.0 */
	char	Info[40];     /* Standard FTSC-1 *.MSG library */
	char	Author[40];   /* (c)1992-1995 Alex May & Nick Loman */
} ;

Each message area is assigned to a message base library. This allows
different libraries to be used with different bases. Therefore, for your
library to know which areas it will be serving, the function "mbInitBase"
will be called for each area, with a pointer to a "struct msgarea *"
configuration structure in A0. This can be used to build-up an internal
list of message pointers, or simply discarded.

If a message area for some reason cannot be initialiased, returning
FALSE will cause it to be unlinked from the internal message area list.


2.0.2 Message Requests


Once these initialisations have occured, your message base library should
be ready to accept requests for manipulating your message database.

The following functions are the minimum required functions for a working
message base library.

struct WholeMessage *    mbLoadMessage ( int, ULONG, ULONG ) ;
signed int               mbSaveMessage ( struct WriteMsgStruct * ) ;
struct MsgScanKey *      mbInitMsgScanKey ( struct ScanInfo * ) ;
ULONG                    mbScanMessages ( struct MsgScanKey *, struct WholeMessage * ) ;
void                     mbCloseMsgScanKey ( struct MsgScanKey * ) ;
signed int               mbEditMessage ( struct Msg *, unsigned char * ) ;
signed int               mbDeleteMessage ( int, ULONG ) ;
ULONG                    mbGetNumberOfMessages ( void ) ;
struct MsgIdx *          mbGetPointers ( ULONG ) ;
int                      mbSetPointers ( struct MsgIdx * ) ;
BOOL			 mbScanMsgHdrs ( struct MsgScanKey *, struct Msg * ) ;

Zeus' message base works under the following principle:

Each message area has a unique "message area number", which can be a minimum
of one and a maximum of ULONG. These message areas are configured from within
Prefs/MsgConfig. No two message area numbers may be the same. Message area
numbers are not in numeric sequence, and can skip from 1 to 5 to 200 etc.

Each message area has some "active data". This active data stores the areas
current lowest message, highest message, high sent by mail processor and high
sent by Internet pointer. You do not need to worry about the last two, but
they must be available for setting by other Zeus programs.

Zeus requires a "struct MsgIdx" to be available from the GetPointers routine
to work out which message it wishes to read next.

struct	MsgIdx
{
	ULONG			 Area;
	ULONG			 Low;
	ULONG			 High;
	ULONG			 HighSentByNet;
	ULONG			 HSBInterNet;
} ;

The Low pointer must be a minimum of one.

This structure is passed to SetPointers in A0 when Zeus wishes to update
an entry itself.


2.0.4 Loading Messages


In order for Zeus to be able to read messages with success, it requires
a universal format for reading.

struct	Msg
{
	ULONG			MsgSection;
	ULONG			Number;
	ULONG			TextPosition;
	ULONG			TextLength;
	char			From[36];
	char			To[36];
	char			Subject[72];
	time_t			Date;
	time_t			ArriveDate;
	struct 	FidoAddress	FromAddress;
	struct  FidoAddress	DestAddress;
	ULONG			ReplyTo;
	ULONG			NextReply;
	ULONG			Flags;
} ;

N.B. You do not have to store your message physically in this format,
but you must translate your message into this structure when you return
messages from mbLoadMessage and mbScanMessages.

A description of fields:

 MsgSection   - The message area number this message belongs to. This
                field is usually the same as the D0 argument passed to
                mbLoadMessage() and the Area field of "struct ScanInfo"
                passed to mbInitMsgScanKey()

 Number       - The message database number. This is usually the same
                as the D1 argument passed to mbLoadMessage(). This number
                relates to the messages position in the areas MsgIdx
                entry.

 TextPosition - This field is used by mbzeus and is not required to be
                filled. However, this may be useful for your library
                to store the position on disk of the message text
                corresponding to the message header.

 TextLength   - The length of the message text minus the NULL terminator,
                i.e. strlen ( wm->wm_Text )

 From         - The author of this message

 To           - The addresse of this message

 Subject      - The "topic" or "title" of this message

 Date         - The date this message was written. The
                zeus.library/Time() and zeus.library/GMTime() functions
                may be useful for creating this field. It represents
                the number of seconds since 01-Jan-1978.

 ArriveDate   - The date this message was stored on the local machine.

 FromAddress  - For echomail and matrix areas- the FidoNet address
                this message originated from.

 DestAddress  - For matrix areas- the destination address of this message.

 ReplyTo      - The previous message in this area linked to the
                current message by MSGID.

 NextReply    - The newt message in this area linked to the current
                message by MSGID.

 Flags        -

#define MI_PRIVATE		0x0001  - Message may only be read by the
                                          author and addresse.
#define MI_FILEATTACHED 	0x0002  - There are file(s) attached to
                                          this message, of which the file-
                                          names are stored in the subject
                                          field.
#define MI_REPLY		0x0004  - This message is a reply to a
                                          previous message.
#define MI_DELETED		0x0008  - This message no longer exists.
#define MI_SENDERANONYMOUS	0x0010  - The sender of this mail is
                                          anonymous
#define MI_RECEIVEANONYMOUS	0x0020  - The addresses of this mail is
                                          shown to be anonymous.
#define MI_DELETEUPONRECEIVE	0x0040  - This message should self
                                          destruct upon read.
#define MI_RECEIVED		0x0080  - This message has been read by
                                          its addresse.
#define MI_CRASH		0x0100  - Matrix bases- this message should
                                          be sent out in CRASH mode.
#define MI_HOLD			0x0200  - Matrix bases- this message should
                                          be sent out in HOLD mode.
#define MI_DIRECT		0x0400  - Matrix bases- this message should
                                          be sent out in DIRECT mode.
#define MI_CROSSREPLY		0x0800  - This message is a reply to a
                                          message in another message base.

This message structure does not include the message text, as this is
often stored in another place to the header. The message text and header
is returned from mbLoadMessage() and mbScanMessage() in the form of a:

struct	 WholeMessage
{
	struct Msg 	 	 wm_Msg;
	UBYTE	        	*wm_Text;
	ULONG			 wm_TextLength;
} ;

Where wm_Text is a pointer to the null-terminated text, and wm_TextLength
is the length of the text allocation. The zmf.library function
FreeMessage() will free these structures like this:

	struct	 WholeMessage	*Msg;

	if ( Msg->wm_Text )
		FreeMem ( Msg->wm_Text, Msg->wm_TextLength ) ;

	FreeMem ( Msg, sizeof ( struct WholeMessage ) ) ;

Therefore, the WholeMessage structure and the Msg->wm_Text field must be
allocated with AllocMem().


2.0.5 Saving Messages


The saving of a message is handled using a "WriteMsgStruct", a structure
which is useful for different applications of saving messages, including
mail processors.

struct      WriteMsgStruct
{
	char			 From[36];
	char			 To[36];
	char			 Subject[72];

	struct 	FidoAddress 	 FromAddress;
	struct 	FidoAddress	 DestAddress;
	struct	FidoAddress	 RouteAddress;

	LONG			 ReplyTo;
	ULONG			 Flags;		/* Standard Message Flags */
	LONG			 MessageArea;

	UBYTE			*FidoText;	/* Use what you like of  */
	UBYTE			*Text;		/* these different texts */
	UBYTE			 Origin[80];    /* Use in this order.    */
	UBYTE			*SeenBys;

	/* Mail Processor Use */

	UBYTE			*FullText;
	long			 FullTextLength;
	BOOL			 NoPointerUpdate;
	time_t			 OriginalTime;
} ;

A description of fields:

 From            - The author of this message

 To              - The addresse of this message

 Subject         - The "topic" or "title" of this message

 FromAddress     - For echomail and matrix areas- the FidoNet address this
                   message originated from.

 DestAddress     - For matrix areas- the FidoNet address this message
                   is destined.

 RouteAddress    - For matrix areas- the FidoNet address this message
                   should be routed through.

 ReplyTo         - The message number this message is a reply to. If this
                   field is 0, the message is an original.

 Flags           - As defined in section 2.0.4

 MessageArea     - The message area number this message should be saved to.


Optional - combinations of the following four fields may be used:

 FidoText        - The FidoNet kludges for this message, or NULL.

 Text            - The main body of the text, or NULL.

 Origin          - For echomail and matrix areas- the Origin text, excluding
                   " * Origin: " prefix and (FidoNet address) suffix.

 SeenBys         - For echomail areas- the SEEN-BY and PATH lines for this
                   text.

Or:

 FullText        - The entire text of the message.

 FullTextLength  - The length of FullText.


 NoPointerUpdate - Message Base library should not update the high
                   message pointers.

 OriginalTime    - The date this message was written.


2.0.6 Database Sharing


void mbUpdate            ( ULONG AreaNumber ) ;
void mbRescan            ( ULONG AreaNumber ) ;
void mbMaintenance       ( ULONG Areanumber ) ;
void mbTrim              ( ULONG AreaNumber ) ;
void mbInitialiseArea    ( ULONG AreaNumber ) ;
void mbCreateWaitingMail ( ULONG AreaNumber ) ;

In order for Zeus to be used effectively with the "starmsg" base, a
few important problems are thrown up.

Zeus shared it's data files using the mbstarmsg.library with another
program, which may have no links whatsoever to Zeus apart from their
reliance on the same data files. How does Zeus know when program "x"
decides to delete a message from the database, how can Zeus keep its
waiting mail data current when program "x" writes it's own message to
the database.

To counter these sharing problems, the "Bin/mbManager" supplied with Zeus
provides links to important functions. All message base libraries should
support the extended functions this program may execute:

   UPDATE       - Check High and Low pointers are correct
   RESCAN       - Scan area for High and Low message pointers
   MAINTENANCE  - Perform a health check on message area(s)
   TRIM         - Trims message area(s) till it contains 'MaxMsgs'
   INITIALISE   - Delete all messages and reset message pointers
   WAITINGMAIL  - Create waiting mail data information from new messages

